    "evidence_text": "Down syndrome (DS) children have a high frequency of acute megakaryoblastic leukemia (AMKL) in early childhood.\n\n2% vs 44.0% +/-\n\n7%) and granulocyte-monocyte (GM) progenitors (15.8% +/-\n\n9%) commensurately reduced.\n\n7% vs 15% +/-\n\nReplating efficiency of CFU-GEMM was also markedly increased.\n\nHemoglobin concentration in white, black, and Oriental children: is there a need for separate criteria in screening for anemia?\n\nExperimental and clinical studies on lactate and pyruvate as indicators of the severity of acute circulatory failure (shock).\n\nA standardized method for production of hemorrhagic shock in the Wistar rat was employed.\n\nPartial correlation analysis demonstrated that neither the measurement of P nor the computation of L/P or XL improved predictability...\n\nFumarase deficiency in dichorionic diamniotic twins.\n\nHeterozygous carriers also carry an increased risk of developing hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma.\n\nTheir clinical phenotype included hepatic involvement.\n\nLactic acidosis in patients with diabetes treated with metformin.\n\nThirty (64 percent) had preexisting cardiac disease, of whom 18 had histories of congestive heart failure. . . .\n\nAssessment of Volume Depletion in Children with Malaria\n\nIntracellular water volume (ICW) was derived from these parameters.\n\nSixteen children had severe and 19 moderate malaria.\n\nThis is defined as mild dehydration in other conditions.\n\nECW measurements were normal on admission in children with severe malaria and did not rise in the first few days of admission.\n\nModerate malaria was not associated with a depletion of TBW.\n\nMutation of the fumarase gene in two siblings with progressive encephalopathy and fumarase deficiency.\n\nThey presented with progressive encephalopathy, dystonia, leucopenia, and neutropenia.\n\nThis substitution occurred in a highly conserved region of the fumarase cDNA.\n\nCurrently there is not a biomarker that can identify HIV-infected people who are at risk for the development of HAND.\n\nThe most striking finding was for the acyl chain of C18\u22360 that consistently associatedwith performance onmultiple tests of memory.\n\n7.2 pmol/h in CFU-E and BFU-E colonies from normal subjects.\n\nChanges in white blood cells and platelets in children with falciparum malaria: relationship to disease outcome.\n\nThe WCC was not associated with a positive blood culture.\n\nIn children with malaria, high lymphocyte and low monocyte counts were independently associated with mortality.\n\nThe mean platelet volume was also higher in children with malaria compared with other medical conditions.\n\nThis may reflect early release from the bone marrow in response to peripheral platelet destruction.",
